import{_ as t,o as a,c as o,a3 as r}from"./chunks/framework.C2zB12QK.js";const p=JSON.parse('{"title":"Release semantics","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"explanation/release-semantics.md","filePath":"explanation/release-semantics.md"}'),s={name:"explanation/release-semantics.md"};function n(i,e,d,c,l,h){return a(),o("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[r('<h1 id="release-semantics" tabindex="-1">Release semantics <a class="header-anchor" href="#release-semantics" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Release semantics&quot;">​</a></h1><p>The semantics below are load-bearing — they are pinned by <strong>ADR-0003</strong> in the org ledger (<code>9aia/project</code>, <code>project/adrs/0003-tarsi-release-semantics-plan-dry-run-bump-tag-manifest.md</code>). This page is the readable summary; the ADR is the contract.</p><h2 id="dry-run-is-the-default" tabindex="-1">Dry-run is the default <a class="header-anchor" href="#dry-run-is-the-default" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Dry-run is the default&quot;">​</a></h2><p><code>tarsi-serve release</code> mutates only with <code>dry_run: false</code> — the <code>--yes</code> equivalent. Orchestration callers (peregrin campaigns, agents) opt in explicitly; anything probing a repo gets plans, never writes. This mirrors the org rule that service surfaces never mutate unless asked.</p><h2 id="bump-inference" tabindex="-1">Bump inference <a class="header-anchor" href="#bump-inference" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Bump inference&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Conventional commits, attributed to units by path prefix. The inference itself is a <strong>strategy</strong> (P-030) — data-driven rules in <code>tarsi.toml</code>&#39;s <code>[release] strategy</code>, defaulting to <code>conventional</code>:</p><table tabindex="0"><thead><tr><th>commit</th><th>bump</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>feat</code></td><td>minor</td></tr><tr><td><code>fix</code> / <code>perf</code></td><td>patch</td></tr><tr><td><code>!</code> / <code>BREAKING CHANGE:</code></td><td>major</td></tr><tr><td><code>chore</code> / <code>docs</code> / <code>test</code> / …</td><td>none — no release</td></tr></tbody></table><p>Bump rules fold <strong>max-match</strong> (a commit contributes the highest matching <code>bump</code>; the unit folds max across commits) against the commit&#39;s <strong>raw type token</strong> — arbitrary types like <code>deps</code> are nameable. Changelog sectioning is the same mechanism: ordered <code>sections[]</code>, first match wins, table order = render order. <code>semantic-release-angular</code> ships semantic-release&#39;s commit-analyzer angular preset as data; <code>keep-a-changelog</code> keeps the same bump rules but renders the six canonical keepachangelog.com sections (<code>### Added</code>/<code>Changed</code>/<code>Deprecated</code>/<code>Removed</code>/<code>Fixed</code>/<code>Security</code>) — KaC is a changelog layout, not a bump policy. Custom tables cover anything else short of non-semver versioning or plugin execution (the documented P-030 boundary).</p><p><code>spec.bump</code> (unit override or explicit unit spec) wins over inference; the strategy&#39;s <code>fallback</code> — filled by <code>release.default_bump</code> when unset — is the repo-wide fallback when inference finds nothing. Commits with empty path lists match every unit — conservative attribution. <code>release.diff_exclude</code> strips matching paths before attribution (a commit left with no paths is dropped).</p><h2 id="per-unit-tags-per-unit-ranges" tabindex="-1">Per-unit tags, per-unit ranges <a class="header-anchor" href="#per-unit-tags-per-unit-ranges" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Per-unit tags, per-unit ranges&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Tag format is <code>{unit}-v{version}</code> per unit (<code>tamias-core-v0.4.2</code>), not repo-level <code>v{semver}</code>. Each unit&#39;s commit range is <code>{latest-tag-for-that-unit}..HEAD</code> — units release independently and only when their own paths changed. <code>params.range</code> overrides with one range for all units.</p><h2 id="manifest-mutation" tabindex="-1">Manifest mutation <a class="header-anchor" href="#manifest-mutation" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Manifest mutation&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Versions are written via <code>toml_edit</code> (<code>tarsi-manifest</code>) — formatting and comments survive. Units with <code>version.workspace = true</code> bump <code>[workspace.package].version</code> instead: <strong>all releasing units in such a workspace share one bump</strong> (last-writer-wins today; per-unit workspace versions are a known limitation).</p><h2 id="ordering-and-rollback" tabindex="-1">Ordering and rollback <a class="header-anchor" href="#ordering-and-rollback" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Ordering and rollback&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The mutation phase is strictly ordered — changelogs → manifests → scoped <code>git add</code> (never <code>-A</code>) → <code>chore(release): bump versions</code> commit → per-unit tags → <strong>push branch and tags</strong> → publish. The push-before-publish order is load-bearing: GitHub Releases auto-creates <code>refs/tags/&lt;tag&gt;</code> at remote HEAD when the tag doesn&#39;t exist yet, which would pin the release to the pre-bump commit.</p><p>Failure handling:</p><ul><li>Any mutation failure → rollback undoes exactly what ran (remote tags deleted, branch reset, local commit/tags removed, created changelog files deleted) and the op reports <code>rolled_back</code>.</li><li>Publish failures roll back <strong>only if nothing published</strong> — once a target succeeded, deleting the tag would orphan a live release, so state is left and the report asks for manual cleanup.</li></ul><h2 id="the-llm-boundary" tabindex="-1">The LLM boundary <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-llm-boundary" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The LLM boundary&quot;">​</a></h2><p>There is deliberately no <code>tarsi-llm</code>. Changelog polish composes with <strong>sepia</strong>: the <em>caller</em> (peregrin node, agent) may prompt a sepia session with the rendered changelog. Conventional-commit output is the only in-repo path — deterministic by construction (ADR-0003, decision 6).</p><h2 id="secrets" tabindex="-1">Secrets <a class="header-anchor" href="#secrets" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Secrets&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Config carries env-var <strong>names</strong> (<code>token_env</code>, <code>CARGO_REGISTRY_TOKEN</code> via cuora secrets records) — never values. The cuora boundary holds end to end: names in manifests, values in the secrets broker.</p><h2 id="vcs-ownership" tabindex="-1">VCS ownership <a class="header-anchor" href="#vcs-ownership" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;VCS ownership&quot;">​</a></h2><p>Git history, tags, and push go through <code>lasius-vcs</code> + <code>lasius-vcs-git</code> (org plan P-019). <code>tarsi-core</code> is pure — it sees only <code>Commit</code> values; every VCS call lives in <code>tarsi-serve</code> (and <code>tarsi-testkit</code> fixtures). New publish targets are a new <code>tarsi-publish-*</code> crate plus a <code>PublishTarget</code> variant; serve dispatch stays a match arm.</p>',23)])])}const m=t(s,[["render",n]]);export{p as __pageData,m as default};
